.TH MUI 2
.SH NAME
Class, Object, Theme, obj, text, button, field, checkmark, group, space,
add, weigh, fit, lay, paint, window_open, window_paint, window_run, Event,
Requester, requester, req_handle, parse_theme \- the widget toolkit
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B import "vsys:libmui"
.PP
.nf
.B "Class :: enum u8 { Space, Text, Button, Checkmark, String, Group }"
.B "Object :: struct { class: Class, horiz: bool, label: string, weight: int,"
.B "    minw, minh, maxw, maxh: int, x, y, w, h: int,"
.B "    first, next, parent: ^Object, on: bool, id: int }"
.B "Theme :: struct { pad, gap, bevel, hpad, vpad, well: int,"
.B "    ground, face, lit, shade, ink: libpal.RGB }"
.B "default_theme : Theme"
.PP
.B "obj :: proc(class: Class) -> ^Object"
.B "text :: proc(label: string) -> ^Object"
.B "button :: proc(label: string) -> ^Object"
.B "field :: proc() -> ^Object"
.B "checkmark :: proc(on: bool) -> ^Object"
.B "group :: proc(horiz: bool) -> ^Object"
.B "space :: proc() -> ^Object"
.B "add :: proc(parent: ^Object, child: ^Object) -> ^Object"
.B "weigh :: proc(o: ^Object, w: int) -> ^Object"
.PP
.B "fit :: proc(o: ^Object, t: ^Theme)"
.B "lay :: proc(o: ^Object, x, y, w, h: int, t: ^Theme)"
.B "paint :: proc(b: []u8, at: int, root: ^Object, dst: u32, f: ^Fonts, t: ^Theme) -> int"
.PP
.B "window_open :: proc(win: ^Window, title: string, root: ^Object) -> bool"
.B "window_paint :: proc(win: ^Window)"
.B "window_run :: proc(win: ^Window)"
.PP
.B "Event :: struct { kind: Event_Kind, x, y: int, buttons: u8, key: u8 }"
.B "Requester :: struct { root, def, cancel: ^Object }"
.B "requester :: proc(message, ok_label, cancel_label: string) -> Requester"
.B "req_handle :: proc(req: ^Requester, ev: Event) -> (done: bool, id: int)"
.PP
.B "parse_theme :: proc(t: ^Theme, text: string)"
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Libmui
is a widget toolkit in the manner of the Amiga's MUI: a program builds a
tree of
.B Object
nodes, says which stretch and which stay a fixed size, and the library
solves for every rectangle.
No call in this package takes a pixel coordinate from its caller except
the one that finally hands a laid-out tree a place to sit.
The look is
.IR chrome (2)'s
"Cyberpunk Workstation 1994" vocabulary; a
.B Theme
carries the metrics
.I fit
and
.I lay
read and the colours
.I paint
reads, and every window shares
.BR default_theme " until a "
.IR theme (6)
file changes it.
.SS Building a tree
.B Obj
allocates a node of the given class from the ring 3 heap and answers
.B nil
on exhaustion, which every other constructor here passes through
unchanged.
.BR Text ", " button ", " field ", " checkmark " and " space
are the leaves:
.I text
is a rigid run of characters,
.I button
a raised control with a label that stretches both ways,
.I field
a recessed one-line text entry that stretches wide,
.I checkmark
a rigid square lamp that is on or off, and
.I space
blank room that stretches without limit, the glue of a layout.
.B Group
is the one container: it lays its children along one axis, left to right
when
.I horiz
is true, top to bottom otherwise.
.B Add
links
.I child
as the last child of
.I parent
and answers
.I parent ,
so a tree reads as nested calls.
.B Weigh
sets a node's share of the room left over on its parent's axis after
every sibling's smallest size is met, and answers the node it was given.
A node whose largest extent equals its smallest never grows and needs no
weight; every other leaf takes a weight of one unless a caller calls
.I weigh
first.
.PP
A label may carry one underscore before a letter, stripped when drawn and
not counted in the label's width, to mark that letter as a hotkey.
.SS Layout
.I Fit
walks a tree once from the leaves up and fills every node's smallest and
largest width and height from its class and, for a
.BR Group ,
from its children.
.I Lay
walks once from the root down: it gives
.I o
the rectangle
.RI ( x , " y" ", " w ", " h ),
and for a group shares the room beyond each child's smallest size out
along the axis in proportion to weight, tiled so the shares sum to the
whole with nothing lost to rounding.
On the other axis each child is offered the group's full inner extent,
clamped to what it accepts, and centred in what is left over.
A caller runs
.I fit
once and then
.I lay
with the outer rectangle it has, in that order; a node's rectangle after
.I lay
is read straight off its
.BR x , " y" , w " and " h
fields.
.SS Painting
.I Paint
walks a laid-out tree and writes the
.IR draw (2)
commands that draw it onto image
.I dst
into buffer
.I b
at offset
.IR at ,
answering the new offset.
It fills the tree's own ground first, then each node: a button's face and
bevel from
.IR chrome (2)'s
vocabulary, a checkmark's lamp, a field's sunken well, and a label's
glyphs blit from an atlas
.I f
already holds.
.I Paint
never uploads a glyph itself; a caller bakes the atlases a tree needs with
.IR font (2)'s
.I font_prepare
before the first paint.
A group draws nothing of its own, only its children.
.SS A live window
.I Window_open
claims a window from
.IR intuition (4),
lays
.I root
out in its client area, bakes the atlases the tree needs, and paints it
once; it answers false at the first step a program cannot have a window
without, and does not return past that point.
.I Window_paint
redraws the whole tree and flushes it.
.I Window_run
is the event loop: a thread reads the window's
.B mouse
file and the caller's own thread its
.BR cons ,
each through an
.IR ioproc (2),
until the window ends.
A press hit-tests the tree and marks the gadget under the pointer; the
matching release, if it lands on the same gadget, activates it, flips a
checkmark's state, and calls
.IR win.handler ,
a program's own
.B "proc(win: ^Window, id: int)" ,
with the gadget's
.BR id .
Tab moves the focus among the tree's interactive gadgets, in tree order,
and wraps past the last back to the first.
Return or space activates the focused gadget, and Escape calls
.I win.handler
with an id of \-1, a program's own signal to cancel.
.SS Requesters
A
.B Requester
is a small modal tree outside a window's own: a message over a row of
buttons pushed right by glue, an
.B OK
button as its default and, when a label is given, a cancel button.
.I Requester
builds this tree and answers it; the caller lays and paints it itself,
centred in whatever rectangle it chooses.
.I Req_handle
applies one
.B Event
to it: Return chooses the default, Escape the cancel, and a release over
a button that button, answering
.I done
true with the chosen button's
.IR id .
A requester is not wired into
.IR window_run ;
a program that opens one drives its own loop over
.IR req_handle
for as long as the requester is up.
.SS The theme
.I Parse_theme
reads a role at a time from a
.IR theme (6)
file's text into a
.B Theme
it starts at
.BR default_theme ,
so a file that names nothing is the chassis look and one that names a
role changes that role alone.
.SH EXAMPLES
A row of two buttons in a window:
.IP
.EX
root := libmui.group(true)
libmui.add(root, libmui.button("_Open"))
libmui.add(root, libmui.button("_Save"))

win: libmui.Window
win.handler = proc "contextless" (win: ^libmui.Window, id: int) {
	if id == -1 {win.done = true}
}
if libmui.window_open(&win, "demo", root) {
	libmui.window_run(&win)
}
.EE
.SH SOURCE
.B sys/libmui
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR draw (2),
.IR chrome (2),
.IR font (2),
.IR ioproc (2),
.IR theme (6),
.IR intuition (4),
.IR muidemo (1)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.I Window_open
answers false for any step that leaves a program without a usable
window: a claim
.IR intuition (4)
refuses, a geometry it cannot lay a tree in, or an atlas pool that fills
before every label is baked.
Nothing else here reports failure; a tree too large for its buffer is
.IR paint 's
own truncation, described in
.IR draw (2).
.SH BUGS
A label's hotkey underscore marks a letter but nothing yet turns a
keystroke of it into an activation; only Tab, Return, Escape and a click
move or press a gadget.
